      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "fed0adfd-66b2-5c00-bc53-ef90207efbda",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Korean Jindo, Taigan, Nureongi\n- Organizations: United Kennel Club, Fédération Cynologique Internationale, national kennel clubs\n- Places: Korea, Kyrgyzstan\n- Concepts: dog breed recognition, sighthounds, spitz dogs\n\nFacts:\n- The Korean Jindo breed was recognized by the United Kennel Club on January 1, 1998.\n- The Korean Jindo breed was recognized by the Fédération Cynologique Internationale in 2005.\n- The Taigan is a sighthound breed from Kyrgyzstan.\n- The Taigan is also known as Kyrgyzdyn Taighany in Kyrgyz and Kyrgyzskaya Borzaya Taigan in Russian.\n- The Taigan is not recognized by the Fédération Cynologique Internationale, but it is recognized by a number of kennel clubs at the national level.\n- Nureongi, described as Korean Yellow spitzes, are medium-sized spitz dogs like the Korean Jindo.\n- Nureongi have greater musculature and distinctive coat patterns compared with native Korean dogs such as the Korean Jindo.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
